Approach 1: Simulation (Your Approach)

Where it comes from: This is the standard way to solve the problem by following the instructions step by step.

Idea: You use a loop to keep playing rounds. If the teams are even, exactly half play. If odd, one gets a free pass and the rest play. You keep doing this until 1 team is left.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    int numberOfMatches(int n) {
        int matches = 0;
        while (n > 1) {
            if (n % 2 == 0) {
                matches = matches + (n / 2);
                n = n / 2;
            } else {
                matches = matches + ((n - 1) / 2);
                n = ((n - 1) / 2) + 1;
            }
        }
        return matches;
    }
};
Time Complexity: O(log n) because the teams halve each time.

Space Complexity: O(1) because it uses only basic variables.

What makes the next approach better: While dividing by two is fast, we can solve this puzzle without any loops at all by looking at what a match actually does in the real world.

Approach 2: The Math Brain Teaser

Where it comes from: This is a classic logic puzzle often found in LeetCode editorials and math competitions.

Idea: Think about the goal of a tournament. You start with n teams and you want to end up with exactly 1 winner. Every single time a match is played, exactly 1 team loses and goes home. To get rid of n - 1 teams so that only 1 winner is left, you must play exactly n - 1 matches.

